From alegedza at caregroup.harvard.edu  Wed Apr  3 18:15:33 2002
From: alegedza at caregroup.harvard.edu
Date: Wed, 03 Apr 2002 13:15:33 -0500
Subject: Summary: forcing zeroes into a table using factor()
Message-ID: <C0CFE404A411D4118FEC00508B0C94E705AB4425@sr12flamingo.caregroup.harvard.edu>

Dear S folks,
Thank to Alan Zaslavsky, Gabriel Baud-Bovy, Jim Pratt, Phil Spector, and
Bill Dunlap for 
suggestions on how to
force zero columns or rows to appear in a table. Alan summarizes one
approach (see
below): use factor() to create preset levels in a table. I used Gabriel's
factor() solution since 
it was easiest for the task at hand, but all the other replies are useful as
well. 
Bill notes that this type of approach is also useful when one desires to
have
an ordering of levels which is non-alphabetical or non-numerical.

Anna 

Replies:
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Alan Zaslavsky:

The table() function by default turns the arguments into factors before
tabulating.  If you instead turn them into factors first by explicit calls
to factor(), you can specify the levels of the factor as an argument to
factor().  That way you are guaranteed that each dimension of the table
always corresponds to the same levels (and of course number of levels)
regardless of the actual data values.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Gabriel Baud-Bovy:

You can use factor():

n<-10
inhaler<-factor(rbinom(n=n, size=1,prob=0.4),levels=0:1)
natural<-factor(rep(0,n),levels=0:1)
data<-data.frame(inhaler,natural)
table(inhaler, natural)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Jim Pratt:

Why not create the tables yourself rather than use table() to do it?

Suppose you consider one set of margins fixed, N1 and N2 (these can be
generated by some other process, but at time of generating table cell,
assume fixed).  Then generate X<-rbinom(N1,px) and Y<-rbinom(N2,py).
Then the table can be created by:

matrix(c(X,N1-X,Y,N2-Y),ncol=2)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Phil Spector:

Perhaps something like this:

tt <- table(inhaler,natural)
if(dim(tt)[1] == 1)tt <- rbind(tt,c(0,0))
if(dim(tt)[2] == 1)tt <- cbind(tt,c(0,0))
mcnemar.test(tt)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Bill Dunlap:

Convert your inputs to factors with those levels before
giving them to table.  If table gets a non-factor it
converts it to a factor with the levels being the unique
values in the vector, but if you give it a factor it
uses whatever levels are in your factor.  E.g.,

   > table(factor(c(0,0,0), levels=0:1), factor(c(1,1,1), levels=0:1))
     0 1
   0 0 3
   1 0 0

Another use for this is when you want a certain order for
the levels which is not alphabetical or numerical order.
(E.g., levels=c("Low","Med","High"))

----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Original post:

Dear S folks,

Simulating many 2x2 tables where the margins 
represent binary/indicator variables. Occasionally, 
