Subject: Summary: Bug in qf() ?
Message-ID: <3CABA9E9.F98DF236@forestresearch.co.nz>

The bug in qf() is a known bug in Splus and will be fixed in version 6.1
(see below). I searched S news and could find no reference to this.

Barney Campbell the Splus distributor in New Zealand has verified its
existence since (at least) Splus 3.4.

Insightful was aware of the bug, but chose not to inform the user
community, which I find disappointing. I believe they should post a
temporary fix right away, given that one can write a version of qf()
using pf() and approx() in a few lines. 

Bugs of this type, where wrong answers are produced (rather than an
error message) should always be reported to users. This is for 2
reasons: 

(i) people could be getting wrong answers: there is nothing worse for a
statistician's integrity and reputation than to give wrong answers to
clients, except for knowing they have given wrong answers and neglecting
(this is called negligence) to disclose and correct a known mistake,
which is what Insighful has done to us.

(ii) time cost for users to track down a bug in their functions--for
this bug alone this would have cost me the equivalent of one year's
maintenance, which I could have saved had the bug been posted. 

I would like to know: why isn't there a list of bugs, and fixes as
Redhat Linux or Microsoft provide?

If any other users of this newsgroup have reported bugs, please post a
summary of the bug report and Insightful's response.

Rod Ball

