Subject: Polynomial contrasts and Type III ANCOVA
Message-ID: <3.0.6.32.20020404140627.0087e470@shaker.med.umn.edu>

Dear S-plus users,

I am fitting the following model (S+2K):

fit<-aov(emg.st ~ emg.b + rep + loc*sp,data)

where emg.b is a covariate, rep and loc are factors and sp is an ordered
factor with three levels. There can be a few missing data in some datasets.
In addition of the main and interaction effects,  I want to know if there 
is a linear or quadratic trend in sp which is easy for SS Type I:

summary(fit,list(sp=list(L=1,Q=2)),ssType=1)

Type III ANOVA is controversial issue but I beleive that it makes 
sense in this case (given the fact that I have a covariate
and some missing values). 

Some experiments with SPSS have shown that the sum of
square associated with the contrasts listed in the output
is always equal to the SS of squares obtained with a SSTYPE III
model:

UNIANOVA emg.st BY loc sp rep WITH emg.b
        /CONTRAST(sp) = POLYNOMIAL
        /METHOD = SSTYPE(3)
        /INTERCEPT = INCLUDE
        /CRITERIA = ALPHA(.05)
        /DESIGN = emg.b rep sp loc loc*sp .

even if I specify a SSTYPE I in the syntax.

UNIANOVA emg.st BY loc sp rep WITH emg.b
        /CONTRAST(sp) = POLYNOMIAL
        /METHOD = SSTYPE(1)
        /INTERCEPT = INCLUDE
        /CRITERIA = ALPHA(.05)
        /DESIGN = emg.b rep sp loc loc*sp .

How can I compute those contrasts with S-PLUS? 

Thank you.

Gabriel Baud-Bovy

