> nrows
[1] 3174068
> ttt <- tmp.double[sample(1:nrows, 500000, rep = F), ]
> memory.size()
[1] 1133854720

Why is it that on NT only 2.7 MB of memory is tied up after the subsetting 
while 1.1 GB of memory is
tied up after the same operation on the Solaris machine?

Another question, maybe related, I am not sure?  As I said previously 
"tmp.double" was ported to Solaris
from NT through "data.dump" and "data.restore", neverthless the same object 
occupies much more space
on the Solaris than on the NT platform, is this normal?  Does this have 
something to do with the fact
that on NT Splus is a 32-bit application while on Solaris it is a 64-bit 
application?

On Solaris:

> dim(tmp.double)
[1] 3174068      35
> object.size(tmp.double)
[1] 947599192

On NT:

> object.size(tmp.double)
[1] 592097352
> dim(tmp.double)
[1] 3174068      35

