From dsmith at insightful.com  Fri Apr  5 18:54:36 2002
From: dsmith at insightful.com (David Smith)
Date: Fri, 05 Apr 2002 10:54:36 -0800
Subject: Re: Splus not freeing it's memory on Solaris
In-Reply-To: <OF228E06AC.5449D2E4-ON85256B92.004F4EE3@spgdag.ca>
Message-ID: <6F270473699FEA419A71961E62EDF9EC0EA7AF@se2kexch01.insightful.com>

> I am wondering why memory is not released after a simple
> subsetting operation?

Many Unix-like operating systems do not allow processes to return memory
to the pool after it's been claimed.  From help(memory.size):

"On most UNIX machines S-PLUS will not shrink its data space when its
requirements decrease. For example under a SUN Solaris, S-PLUS cannot
shrink the data space so memory.size() will return the same answer
regardless of the value of maximum. Under Windows NT/2000, if it is able
to shrink the data space, it will try to shrink it at the end of each
top level expression. "

You'll notice on Solaris that subsequent operations do not increase the
memory size, as they use the unused memory held by the S-PLUS process:

> dim(tmp.double) #only 5 columns for quicker response
[1] 3174063       5
> memory.size()  #largest memory ever allocated by S+
[1] 167559168
> ttt <- tmp.double[sample(1:nrows, 500000, rep = F), ]      
> memory.size()
[1] 208527360

# Memory does not grow when additinal operations
# do not require more memory than all previous operations

> ttt2 <- tmp.double[sample(1:nrows, 500000, rep = F), ]
> memory.size()                                         
[1] 208535552
> ttt3 <- tmp.double[sample(1:nrows, 500000, rep = F), ]   
> memory.size()                                         
[1] 208535552

> the same object occupies much more space
> on the Solaris than on the NT platform, is this normal?

64-bit objects are larger than 32-bit objects. You can see the
difference by comparing 32-bit and 64-bit versions of S-PLUS on Solaris:

Version 6.0.4 Release 1 for Sun SPARC, SunOS 5.6 : 2001 
Working data will be in /homes/dsmith/MySwork 
> object.size(1:10)  ## 32-bit vector, storage mode "integer"
[1] 81
> object.size(1:10+0.1) ## 32-bit vector, storage mode "double"
[1] 121

Version 6.0.4 Release 1 for Sun SPARC, SunOS 5.8, 64-bit : 2001 
Working data will be in /homes/dsmith/MySwork 
> object.size(1:10) ## 64-bit vector, storage mode "integer"
[1] 161
> object.size(1:10+0.1) ## 64-bit vector, storage mode "double"
[1] 161

# David Smith

