From gabriel at shaker.med.umn.edu  Mon Apr  8 15:40:36 2002
From: gabriel at shaker.med.umn.edu (Gabriel Baud-Bovy)
Date: Mon, 08 Apr 2002 17:40:36 +0200
Subject: Contrasts in unbalanced designs
Message-ID: <3.0.6.32.20020408174036.00886100@shaker.med.umn.edu>

I am including below the function contrast.test() which computes and tests
contrasts with unbalanced designs. I also include a data set and some
examples. However, I am not sure that the function is correct since
I found out that SPSS and BMDP 2V compute simple polynomial contrasts
differently on this dataset.  BMDP always uses the values of the 
marginal means while SPSS uses the  adjusted means (which depends on 
the analysis). 

The function contrast.test can reproduce the BMDP results with the argument
type="marginal.means". It uses the s-plus function se.contrast() 
to estimate the standard error. It can also estimate the contrasts 
like SPSS with the argument type="adj.means" but I was unable to 
find out how SPSS computed the standard error. I report the
results of several analyses below.

I would very much appreciate your opinion on the following questions:
For unbalanced designs, should one use marginal means or adjusted means?
Is the definitions of the standard error in BMDP (and SPLUS) correct for
all the cases illustrated below? does any body know how SPSS computes
the standard error?

Any comment on the function would also be welcome.

Sincerely,

Gabriel

I can illustrate the differences between SPSS and BMDP with the small data
set that is provided at the end of this email. This data set has one dependent
variable (y), 2 grouping variables (x and b) with 3 levels each, and
one covariate (c). The data are unbalanced:

   B1 B2 B3 
A1  2  2  2
A2  2  2  2
A3  3  3  2

I computed polynomial contrasts for x (linear and quadratic) for the 
following designs:

y~x 
y~b+x 
y~b+x+b:x
y~b+x+b:x+c

and I came to the conclusion that BMDP always use the values of the 
marginal means for x (i.e. 26.67, 44.33, 54.0) while SPSS uses the 
adjusted means (which depends on the analysis).

The standard error used in the t.test of the contrast depends on the
design. The standard error computed by BMDP and SPSS are different but
the difference is not very large for this dataset except when the 
covariate was involved. Both programs give the same (Type III) 
analysis of variance table.

# data set

data<-data.frame(y=c(23,12,25,53,34,13,42,23,56,45,67,
                33,85,21,43,45,63,34,54,87),
        x=ordered(rep(1:3,c(6,6,8))))
data$b<-factor(rep(c("b1","b2","b3"),7)[1:20])
data$c<-1:nrow(data)

  y x b  c 
 23 1 1  1
 12 1 2  2
 25 1 3  3
 53 1 1  4
 34 1 2  5
 13 1 3  6
 42 2 1  7
 23 2 2  8
 56 2 3  9
 45 2 1 10
 67 2 2 11
 33 2 3 12
 85 3 1 13
 21 3 2 14
 43 3 3 15
 45 3 1 16
 63 3 2 17
 34 3 3 18
 54 3 1 19
 87 3 2 20

#
# y~x
#

aov.obj<-aov(y~x,data,qr=T)

# results of BMDP and SPSS (there is no difference betwee
contrast.test(aov.obj,"x",data,type="marginal.means")                  
           SS  Estimates StdError          t resid.df          P 
