References: <3CB31751.4832965A@psyres-stuttgart.de>
Message-ID: <5.1.0.14.0.20020409093402.02f05200@uidaho.edu>

Bernd,

this is a perennial problem with communications in statistics.  I advise 
students and colleagues to avoid R2, because it is only a reliable estimate 
if the residuals and fitted values are bivariate normal (see e.g. Cook and 
Weisberg 1999).

I find reporting the standard deviation of the residuals more useful in 
linear models, and in the case of hierarchical models, the covariance 
matrix of the random effects as well.  It's not as easy to understand as 
R2, but it's more complete.  It's also a little problematic if the data are 
transformed, and I haven't thought of an easy way around that.

Cook, R. D. and S. Weisberg (1999). Applied regression including computing 
and graphics, Wiley Interscience.

Andrew

