The ecology literature is far from being alone in having an allergy to 
insignificant variables in a regression model.  And the m/10 rule of thumb 
(some people use m/20; m=effective sample size) means that entertaining (not 
necessarily fitting, but any analysis that includes Y) more than m/10 
parameters may result in unreliable predictions, especially from the standpoint 
of absolute calibration accuracy  and lesser from the standpoint of 
overstatement of R^2.

It's worth contemplating how many nearly unbiased estimators of sigma^2 in 
ordinary linear models have been proposed.  I can think of two.  First, there's 
MSE/(n-p-1), where p is the number of parameters entertained, even if some have 
been eliminated through examining associations with Y.  Second, Ye proposed a 
"data mining corrected sigma^2" where he estimates p from a pertubation 
analysis in which Y is molested slightly and changes in the final model are 
analyzed.  In Ye's examples, the value of p he estimated was about 3/4 of the 
way from the number of parameters in the final fit towards the number of 
parameters entertained.

Frank Harrell

