> "NetSolarRad" = ~ 1 + poly(NetSolarRad) + poly(NetSolarRad,2) ,
> "TWI" = ~ 1 + poly(TWI) + poly(TWI,2) + poly(TWI,3)).....
> 
> would the df that I have to consider used for GeoNutrients be 2 (the 
> highest considered term, or 3 (both 1 for the linear and 2 for the quadratic)?

df = 2 (higheset complexity entertained)

> 
> P values:
> I guess I thought the p value truly means nothing. I suppose it is acting 
> as a screen, but isn't it relatively undefined in this circumstance? The 
> true p of the F test isn't really known, is it? I thought that the process 
> of selection influences the inference, so I would assume that is also true 
> for the p of the Chisq.

Right.  In the simplest case of stepwise selection when all variables are 
linear the test statistics do not have the stated distributions, so P-values 
are wrong.
Laurence Freedman showed some time ago that using the MSE from the fullest 
model thoughout the stepwise process fixes a good bit of the problem.

