From lambert.winifred at ensco.com  Tue Apr 16 17:51:19 2002
From: lambert.winifred at ensco.com (Lambert.Winnie)
Date: Tue, 16 Apr 2002 13:51:19 -0400
Subject: Summary: Predict.gam() with polynomials
Message-ID: <8986151694190742869D08450EE4DCDE08ECD0@amu-exch.ensco.win>

I only got one response from Nick Ellis, but what a response it was.  It was so 
good that I am leaving it intact and forwarding it on to the group - see below. 
 Thanks, Nick.  

-----Original Message-----
From: Nick.Ellis at csiro.au [mailto:Nick.Ellis at csiro.au] 
Sent: Tuesday, April 16, 2002 1:06 AM
To: Lambert.Winnie
Cc: bugs at insightful.com
Subject: RE: [S] Predict.gam() with polynomials

Try 

new.poly <- lm(newPrm ~ poly(newSpd, 2), data=na.omit(new.df))

This makes predict.gam behave as expected.

Your version

new.poly <- lm(newPrm ~ poly(newSpd, 2), data=new.df, na.action=na.exclude)

probably works for missing explanatory variables, but not for missing response 
variable.

The help file says:
"na.action 
a function to filter missing data. This is applied to the model.frame after any 
subset argument has been applied. The default is na.fail, which returns an 
error if any missing values are found. An alternative is na.exclude, which 
deletes observations that contain one or more missing values. "

It's not clear that this works for explanatory variables only so it looks to me 
like a bug.

More details follow...

It seems that inside predict.gam the information that the response variable was 
missing in the original fit is not passed down. In particular the line

        refit <- lm.wfit(X[nx1 > 0,  , drop = F], pred, w, method, singular.ok 
= T)
        
has (seen inside the browser)

b(safe.predict.gam)> length(w)
[1] 19
b(safe.predict.gam)> nrow(X[nx1>0,])
[1] 26

The problem is inside lm.wfit, x and w.factor are not commensurate. 

> traceback()
8: eval(action, sys.parent())
7: doErrorAction("Problem in x * w.factor: length of longer operand (78) should 
be a multiple of length of shorter (19)" ,
6: x * w.factor
5: lm.wfit(X[nx1 > 0,  , drop = F], pred, w, method, singular.ok = T)
4: safe.predict.gam(object, newdata, type, se.fit, terms, evalFrame =
safeEvalFrame)
3: predict.gam(new.poly, na.omit(new.df))
2: eval(expression(predict.gam(new.poly, na.omit(new.df))))
1: 
Message: Problem in x * w.factor: length of longer operand (78) should be a 
multiple of length of shorter (19) 

This arises because inside safe.predict.gam, nrow(X[nx1>0,]) != length(w). This 
is because X is created from this code

        m <- model.frame(Terms, data, na.action = na.action)
        X <- model.matrix(Terms, m)
        
but data does not include the response variable, only the explanatory 
variables, so the na.action does nothing.

