Hi,

My program is a simple loop function that generates for each pass "nt" multivariate normal vectors. I assign this random sample to the variable r, calculate for each line a weighted mean, put this result in a variable rp and calculate the standard deviation and the quantile 1% of this vector rp, results that I assign to the vectors std and q1 respectively (note that I allocated memory for these vectors before the loop). When I run my program (typically, a covariance matrix of dimension 300, 100 experiments with nt=100), memory is growing at each iteration and my system is eventually running low in virtual memory. How can I avoid that? I also tried to "plug" the rmvnorm call directly in the sort statement (since I use the "r" result just once) but the memory growing problem still persists. 

Any hint is greatly appreciated!

Thank you very much, Patrick

P.S. I'm using S-Plus 4.5 under Windows NT. All the arguments of my function have default values so you can try it by the simple call simulate().

simulate _ function(V=diag(300), w = rep(1/ncol(V), ncol(V)), nt = 100, nexps = 100)

{

        std <- vector(nexps, mode = "numeric")

        q1 <- vector(nexps, mode = "numeric")

        q1rnk <- round(nt * 0.01, 0)

        for(i in 1:nexps) {

                r <- rmvnorm(n = nt, cov = V, d = nrow(V))

                rp <- sort(apply(r, 1, function(rend, wgts) t(wgts) %*% rend, w))

                std[i] <- sqrt(var(rp))

                q1[i] <- rp[q1rnk]

                remove(c("r", "rp"), frame = 2)

        }

        return(std)

}

