From AHUMBOLT at samson.com  Thu Apr 18 21:51:22 2002
From: AHUMBOLT at samson.com (Allen Humbolt)
Date: Thu, 18 Apr 2002 16:51:22 -0500
Subject: Summary of Question about Modeling with Constraints
Message-ID: <A851C2B696F08B4EB5A0C9E867A1B7920181F196@tulex1.samson.local>

I thank Brian Ripley and Leonid Gibiansky for answering my question.

THE ORIGINAL QUESTION:
I would like to fit Y = b0 + b1*X1 + b2*X2 + . . . + b12*X12
and estimate the coefficients (b's) subject to the constraints
 b1+ b2+ b3 + . . . + b11 + b12 = 1   and
 b1 > b2 > b3 > . . . > b11 > b12
Is there a way to do this in Splus or an available Splus library?

SOLUTION:
I implented Professor Ripley's solution, so I'll describe it.  If I have
time to work through Leonid's solution, I'll summarize it later.  First, I
needed to know that the MASS library contains the function nnls.fit, a
linear least squares function which constrains all coefficients to be
non-negative.  [In my edition of V&R it's described in Section 9.6]  Second,
I had to know to reparametrize by the successive differences and eliminate
my b12 term above.  The 11 parameter estimates of the differences (obtained
from nnls.fit), plus the constraint that the parameters add up to 1, gives
me 12 equations and 12 unknowns, which I can solve to get coefficients back
to their original desired meaning.  I used Excel Solver to accomplish this
quite easily.  I could also have set it up as a regression problem to solve
(with no intercept and no error).  If getting back to my original
parametrization is more obvious than this, I welcome that feedback.  For
those who don't have V&R I'll just briefly describe that the nnls.fit
function expects the predictor variables in a matrix X and the response in a
vector y so that the statement is something like:
 myfit <- nnls.fit(X, y)
The nnls.fit function does not automatically include an intercept term.  It
must be added if needed (and I needed it).

Thanks again for the help, Brian Ripley and Leonid Gibiansky.  For those who
wish to see the actual responses from Brian and Leonid, I include those at
the bottom.

Allen Humbolt
Quantitative Analyst, Samson
ahumbolt at samson.com

Brian D. Ripley

"reparametrize by the differences, eliminate b12 and use nnls."

Leonid Gibiansky

You may try to achieve this via reparametization. E.g. for

b(i) > 0 (I assume you need > 0 restriction ?)  you may introduce

a(1), a2,.. a(12) and

b(1)=a(1)^2/(b1+b2+..+..b12)
b(i)=(b(i-1)+a(i)^2)/(b1+b2+..+..b12)

(compute b(i) via a(i) on the right hand side)

Then fit a(i)

