From therneau at mayo.edu  Mon Apr 22 12:52:07 2002
From: therneau at mayo.edu (Terry Therneau)
Date: Mon, 22 Apr 2002 07:52:07 -0500
Subject: Re: Simultaneous use of cluster() and frailty() in coxph()?
In-Reply-To: <1508FB10AFDBD211854D0090273C410708A1FB11@com83-178.medicine.uiowa.edu>
Message-ID: <200204221252.g3MCq7X18786@rocky.mayo.edu>

The short answer is no, you cannot use both a frailty and a cluster term in
coxph or survReg.

The longer answer:
  1. I am not sure that it makes sense to do so.  The cluster() argument
asks for a GEE-type sandwich estimate of variance, the frailty() term for
a mixed model.  Choose one modeling approach or the other.

  2. You have a data set with nested random effects.  The coxph routine,
via frailty, can only do a single level of random effect.  If all goes well,
there may be a first version, here at Mayo, that can do multi-level effects
within the next 2 weeks.  But mind the "if" ...

  3. Your best bet at this time is to bootstrap the frailty fit.  
     - yes, it will take a lot of CPU time
     - and some intellectual time to think through the resampling plan, since
     you have to resample people not observations
     - you also have to make sure that copies of the same person, within the
     resampled data set, have a different value for the "id" variable used
     within the frailty(id) term.  See page 249 of Therneau and Grambsch,
     Modeling Survival Data, for an example.
     
  4. I actually never expected users to try both types of terms at once.  (I
know, I should expect ANYTHING that can be typed to occur sometime.)  Because
of the sparse computation trick required for frailty terms with a large number
of levels, various downstream parts have to take special actions.   No such
was written in for the "cluster" part of the code, and it gets confused
over whether there are p or p-1 covariates.  Hence the non-intuitive error
message.
        
        
                Terry Therneau
                

