From phillip.stafford at motorola.com  Tue Apr 23 08:21:59 2002
From: phillip.stafford at motorola.com (Stafford Phillip-APS095)
Date: Tue, 23 Apr 2002 03:21:59 -0500
Subject: summary for efficient split
Message-ID: <07077F6C2A38D511920800D0B7821B3204023039@il06exm01.corp.mot.com>

Thanks to David Smith and Tim Hesterberg of Insightful, Charles Berry, Nick 
Ellis (as always) and Eric Elguero for coming up with some interesting 
alternatives to split.  Here are some much less memory-intensive and far more 
efficient methods for splitting:
(note: Each of these methods can exceed split()'s limit by several 
thousand-fold, however in certain circumstances each of these methods, when 
used at the limit of physical/virtual memory cause their own unique and 
interesting errors in S+ for Windows. Tim's method causes the program to 
completely quit without any warning when it hits the memory wall, while Nick's 
method will yield "unable to assign requested dynamic memory" and David's will 
produce errors at inconsistant times during processing, when using the same 
dataset.  Unfortunaltely these errors are not 100% repeatable, but that's the 
problem when working with 100,000,000 data points.)

start here: 
A111    1000
A111    1100
A111    1200
B123    2000
B123    2100
B123    2200
 
wind up here:
A111    1000    1100    1200
B123    200     2100    2200
_________________________ 

let a1 be your data frame
a2_cbind(unique(a1[,1]),matrix(a1[,2],ncol=3,byrow=T))

This assumes a1 is sorted according to its first column.
Eric Elguero
_________________________________

Matrix indexing can be used. The factor V1 can be used to index the rows and 
you need to create an index (df$seq) for the columns. The code to create seq 
relies on the data being already sorted. 
df
    V1   V2 
1 A111 1000
2 A111 1100
3 A111 1200
4 B123 2000
5 B123 2100
6 B123 2200
mat <- matrix(0,2,3)
nseq <- seq(6)
change <- nseq[c(T,df[-1,1] != df[-6,1])]
df$seq <- 1+nseq-change[df[,1]]
df
    V1   V2 seq 
1 A111 1000   1
2 A111 1100   2
3 A111 1200   3
4 B123 2000   1
5 B123 2100   2
6 B123 2200   3
mat[cbind(df[,1],df$seq)] <- df[,2]
mat
     [,1] [,2] [,3] 
[1,] 1000 1100 1200
[2,] 2000 2100 2200

Nick Ellis
________________________________

Assume the column names are "a" and "b".  Try:
N _ nrow(x)
indices _ seq(1, N, by=3)
data.frame(a = x$a[indices],
           b1 = x$b[indices],
           b2 = x$b[indices+1],
           b3 = x$b[indices+2])

You could also try the following, though it skips the error checking in 
data.frame(), use at your own risk:
y _ list(a = x$a[indices],
         b1 = x$b[indices],
         b2 = x$b[indices+1],
         b3 = x$b[indices+2])
attr(y, "row.names") _ 1:(N/3)
oldClass(y) _ "data.frame"

Tim Hesterberg
_____________________________________

N <- nrow(df)
id <- df[seq(1,N,by=3,1])
dat <- matrix(df[,2], ncol=3, byrow=T)
split.df <- data.frame(id, dat)

David Smith

