From sundard at PDF.COM  Wed Apr 24 21:30:12 2002
From: sundard at PDF.COM (Sundar Dorai-Raj)
Date: Wed, 24 Apr 2002 16:30:12 -0500
Subject: Re: na.strings in data.frame()
References: <B47AB4D6EFC8D5118E960002A51342EC01CEF3F6@EXCHNY35.ny.ssmb.com>
Message-ID: <3CC723E4.1EF3AA41@pdf.com>

Sean,

  Here's what I think is going on. I may be way off.
> 
> out <- data.frame(cbind(labs, reg))

If reg is a numeric matrix, cbind(labs,reg) will return a matrix. Since
a matrix can only be one data type, reg will be converted to character.
See this example:

# BEGIN EXAMPLE #
col <- matrix(c(rnorm(20),rep(NA,10)),ncol=1)
lab <- c(rep("normal",20),letters[1:10])
out <- data.frame(cbind(lab,col))

> sapply(out, mode)
       lab      X1.2 
 "numeric" "numeric"
> sapply(out, data.class)
      lab     X1.2 
 "factor" "factor"
# END EXAMPLE #

Now here's the fix:

# BEGIN FIX #
col <- matrix(c(rnorm(20),rep(NA,10)),ncol=1)
lab <- c(rep("normal",20),letters[1:10])
out <- data.frame(lab,col)

> sapply(out, mode)
       lab       col 
 "numeric" "numeric"
> sapply(out, data.class)
      lab       col 
 "factor" "numeric"
# END FIX #

mode(out[,lab]) returns "numeric" because lab is of type "factor". I
usually use data.class to determine type.

Sundar

